arXiv:2509.09001v1 [cs.LG] 10 Sep 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jingwen Liu
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Department of Computer Science, Columbia University, New York, NY, USA
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Hantao Yu
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Department of Computer Science, Columbia University, New York, NY, USA
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Clayton Sanford
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Google Research, San Franciso, CA, USA
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Alexandr Andoni
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Department of Computer Science, Columbia University, New York, NY, USA
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Daniel Hsu
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Department of Computer Science, Columbia University, New York, NY, USA
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Transformers have the representational capacity to simulate Massively Parallel Computation (MPC) algorithms, but they suffer from quadratic time complexity, which severely limits their scalability. We introduce an efficient attention mechanism called Approximate Nearest Neighbor Attention (ANNA) with sub-quadratic time complexity. We prove that ANNA-transformers (1) retain the expressive power previously established for standard attention in terms of matching the capabilities of MPC algorithms, and (2) can solve key reasoning tasks such as Match2 and <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-hop with near-optimal depth. Using the MPC framework, we further prove that constant-depth ANNA-transformers can simulate constant-depth low-rank transformers, thereby providing a unified way to reason about a broad class of efficient attention approximations.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The transformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaswani et al. (2017)</a></cite> has become the dominant neural architecture in deep learning due to its ability to select and compose complex information structures from large inputs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Achiam et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">GeminiTeam et al. (2023)</a></cite>, which in turn enables capabilities such as “in-context learning” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al. (2020)</a></cite> that are crucial for downstream applications.
At the core of transformers is the attention mechanism, which leverages parallelism to gain important advantages over non-parallel architectures (e.g., recurrent nets), including training stability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Miller and Hardt (2019)</a></cite> and representational power <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanford et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Merrill et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanford et al. (2024c)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jelassi et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bhattamishra et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al. (2025)</a></cite>.
One recently highlighted advantage comes from a coarse theoretical relationship between transformers and the Massively Parallel Computation (MPC) model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karloff et al. (2010)</a></cite> that captures the power of large-scale distributed computing frameworks like MapReduce <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dean and Ghemawat (2008)</a></cite>: efficient MPC algorithms can be simulated by small-size transformers, and vice versa <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanford et al. (2024c)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanford et al. (2024a)</a></cite>.
This correspondence suggests that a broad class of computational tasks can be efficiently solved by transformers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Despite these advantages, transformers suffer from a key limitation: the quadratic time complexity of (standard) attention with respect to input size, which is likely to be unavoidable in the worst-case <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Keles et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alman and Song (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alman and Yu (2025)</a></cite>.
To address this limitation, a growing body of work proposes alternatives to the attention mechanism that are more computationally efficient (e.g., sub-quadratic time).
These alternatives employ a variety of techniques, ranging from low-rank approximation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Choromanski et al. (2021)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kacham et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arora et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2021)</a></cite> to efficient nearest neighbor search <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kitaev et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zandieh et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Roy et al. (2021)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Although many of these techniques show promising empirical performance, it is unclear whether they preserve the representational advantages of attention.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we study a particular efficient (sub-quadratic time) attention mechanism, called Approximate Nearest Neighbor Attention (ANNA), and we prove that ANNA retains key representational advantages of standard attention over non-parallel architectures.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We additionally prove that ANNA-transformers can simulate a class of attention mechanisms based on low-rank approximation.
In doing so, we provide a unified way to reason about low-rank and nearest neighbor approaches to efficient attention.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1 </span>Standard transformers and MPC</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanford et al. (2024c)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanford et al. (2024a)</a></cite> established a coarse relationship between standard transformers and MPC by proving the following (for any constant <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>):</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For any MPC algorithm with inputs of size <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> that uses <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> rounds (of computation and communication), <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(N)</annotation></semantics></math> machines, and <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(N^{\varepsilon})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>ε</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(N^{\varepsilon})</annotation></semantics></math> words of local memory per machine (for some constant <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>), there is an equivalent transformer of width <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(N^{\varepsilon+\delta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(N^{\varepsilon+\delta})</annotation></semantics></math> and depth <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(R)</annotation></semantics></math>.
(By width, we mean number of heads per layer times the embedding dimension.)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For any transformer operating on inputs of size <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> that has <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> layers and width <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(N^{\varepsilon})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>ε</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(N^{\varepsilon})</annotation></semantics></math> (for some constant <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>), there is an equivalent MPC algorithm that uses <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(L)</annotation></semantics></math> rounds, <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(N^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(N^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> machines, and <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(N^{\varepsilon+\delta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(N^{\varepsilon+\delta})</annotation></semantics></math> words of local memory per machine.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Notice that the “loss” in the number of rounds or depth is only a constant factor, and the “loss” in the local memory size or width is only a <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(N^{\delta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>δ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(N^{\delta})</annotation></semantics></math> factor (where <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> can be arbitrarily small).
Consequently, these results were sufficient to give new results about transformer representational power (e.g., for various graph reasoning tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanford et al. (2024c)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanford et al. (2024a)</a></cite>), and also give a (conditional) logarithmic-depth lower bound for transformers that solve a multi-hop reasoning problem called <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-hop <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanford et al. (2024c)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, there is a important gap in the MPC algorithm for simulating a transformer (<a href="#S1.I1.i2" title="In 1.1 Standard transformers and MPC ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Item</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> above): <em id="S1.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">it may use up to <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> machines</em>.
This gap suggests the possibility that transformers are strictly more powerful than MPC algorithms, so the relationship established in prior work is very coarse.
Moreover, this gap is likely to be inevitable.
Indeed, when the local computation in an MPC algorithm is fast (say, polynomial-time), and the local memory size is <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>ε</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> for small <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, then a single round of MPC on <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> machines can be simulated on a sequential machine in roughly <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="MN^{O(\varepsilon)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">MN^{O(\varepsilon)}</annotation></semantics></math> time.
However, evaluation of attention over <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> inputs is believed to require at least <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{2-\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{2-\delta}</annotation></semantics></math> time (for every constant <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>) on a sequential machine <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Keles et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alman and Song (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alman and Yu (2025)</a></cite>.
So any such MPC algorithm that simulates attention in <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> rounds should use <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{2-\delta-O(\varepsilon)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{2-\delta-O(\varepsilon)}</annotation></semantics></math> machines.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This gap naturally leads us to the following question: Is there an alternative attention mechanism that more tightly captures the computational power of MPC?</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2 </span>Our contributions</h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We prove that ANNA-transformers more sharply capture the power of MPC algorithms than standard transformers do, and in particular avoid the aforementioned gap in prior works’ characterization of transformers using MPC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanford et al. (2024c)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanford et al. (2024a)</a></cite>.
The core ANNA mechanism is designed to perform <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>-approximate nearest neighbor search <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kushilevitz et al. (1998)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Indyk and Motwani (1998)</a></cite> for a given set of <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> “queries” against a database of <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> “key-value” pairs.
Here <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> is a parameter of the ANNA mechanism that, for the purpose of this present informal description, should be thought of as being at least a large positive constant (e.g., <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\geq 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\geq 10</annotation></semantics></math>).
Throughout, <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the input size.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Informal version of <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1 (ANNA simulates MPC). ‣ 4.1 ANNA-transformer can simulate MPC ‣ 4 Efficient transformers and MPC ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorems</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> and <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 4.2 (MPC simulates ANNA). ‣ 4.2 MPC can simulate ANNA-transformer ‣ 4 Efficient transformers and MPC ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>)<span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix any constants <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon,\delta\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon,\delta\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and ANNA parameter <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>.
For any <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-round MPC algorithm with <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>ε</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> words of local memory, there is an equivalent <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(R)</annotation></semantics></math>-layer ANNA-transformer of width <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(N^{\varepsilon+\delta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(N^{\varepsilon+\delta})</annotation></semantics></math>.
For any <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-layer ANNA-transformer with width <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>ε</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>, there is an equivalent <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(L)</annotation></semantics></math>-round MPC algorithm that uses <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{\varepsilon+\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{\varepsilon+\delta}</annotation></semantics></math> words of local memory and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{1-\delta+O(1/c^{2})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{1-\delta+O(1/c^{2})}</annotation></semantics></math> machines.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Observe that <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1 (Informal version of and ). ‣ 1.2 Our contributions ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> essentially mirrors <a href="#S1.I1.i1" title="In 1.1 Standard transformers and MPC ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Items</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#S1.I1.i2" title="Item 2 ‣ 1.1 Standard transformers and MPC ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> in <a href="#S1.SS1" title="1.1 Standard transformers and MPC ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> except for the resources needed by the MPC algorithm in <a href="#S1.I1.i2" title="In 1.1 Standard transformers and MPC ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Item</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>: the local memory stays the same, but the number of machines required can be strongly sub-quadratic (and in fact, close to linear) in <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>.
This gives a positive answer to the question at the end of <a href="#S1.SS1" title="1.1 Standard transformers and MPC ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We also study efficient attention mechanisms based on low-rank approximation, and by leveraging <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1 (Informal version of and ). ‣ 1.2 Our contributions ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>, we prove that ANNA-transformers have at least the same representational power.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Informal version of <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4.4 (ANNA simulates low-rank attention). ‣ 4.3 ANNA-transformer can simulate low-rank transformers ‣ 4 Efficient transformers and MPC ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>)<span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-layer </span>low-rank attention<span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> transformer, there is an equivalent <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(L)</annotation></semantics></math>-layer ANNA-transformer (of comparable width).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we illustrate the power of ANNA-transformers on two concrete reasoning tasks (Match2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanford et al. (2023)</a></cite> and <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-hop induction heads <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bietti et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanford et al. (2024c)</a></cite>).
We give theoretical constructions of ANNA transformers for these tasks that nearly match the efficiency achievable by standard transformers (<a href="#S5.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 5.2. ‣ 5.1 ANNA-transformer solves Match2 ‣ 5 ANNA-transformer for reasoning tasks ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorems</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> and <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 5.6. ‣ 5.2 ANNA-transformer solves 𝑘-hop ‣ 5 ANNA-transformer for reasoning tasks ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.6</span></a>), and we also show empirically that ANNA-transformers can be trained to approximately solve these tasks (<a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3 Experiments on Match2 and induction heads ‣ 5 ANNA-transformer for reasoning tasks ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.3 </span>Other related works</h3>

<div id="S1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The representational power of standard attention is relatively well understood from a variety of perspectives.
This includes: universal approximation properties in the large depth/width limit <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yun et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Merrill et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Giannou et al. (2023)</a></cite>, ability to recognize formal languages <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hahn (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bhattamishra et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al. (2021)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Strobl et al. (2023)</a></cite>, computational bounds in terms of circuit classes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hao et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Merrill and Sabharwal (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2024)</a></cite> and other parallel computation models (discussed below) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanford et al. (2024c)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanford et al. (2024a)</a></cite>, and bounds for specific compositional tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bietti et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanford et al. (2024b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kozachinskiy (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2024)</a></cite>.
In general, these and other prior works do not consider the representational power of transformers based on efficient (sub-quadratic time) alternatives to attention.
The exceptions are works that give lower bounds for low-rank attention and sparse attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanford et al. (2024c)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2024)</a></cite>, sequential architectures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanford et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jelassi et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bhattamishra et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Merrill et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al. (2025)</a></cite>, more generally, any mechanism that can be evaluated in sub-quadratic time <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Keles et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alman and Song (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alman and Yu (2025)</a></cite>.
What is missing in these prior works, however, is a characterization (and, in particular, upper bounds) for an efficient architecture.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Massively Parallel Computation (MPC) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karloff et al. (2010)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Beame et al. (2017)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goodrich et al. (2011)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Andoni et al. (2014)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Im et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ghaffari (2019)</a></cite> is a model of parallel computing intended to capture the power of MapReduce <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dean and Ghemawat (2008)</a></cite> and other large-scale distributed computing frameworks.
Many works, including those that originally helped to define the MPC model, gave efficient algorithms for a variety of basic data and graph processing tasks.
A connection between MPC and circuit models was given by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Roughgarden et al. (2018)</a></cite> and used to formalize a barrier on proving lower bounds for certain graph problems (e.g., connectivity) in MPC.
Nevertheless, certain conjectured lower bounds in MPC are widely believed (e.g., the 1-vs-2 cycle conjecture <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Im et al. (2023)</a></cite>) and have been used to establish conditional lower bounds for other problems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ghaffari et al. (2019)</a></cite>.
The same conjectured lower bounds have also been used to establish depth lower bounds for transformers via the aforementioned relationship between MPC and transformers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanford et al. (2024c)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanford et al. (2024a)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our main result for ANNA-transformers has an analogue in the context of message-passing graph neural networks (GNNs).
The computational power of such GNNs is characterized by the CONGEST model of distributed computing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peleg (2000)</a></cite> (a refinement of LOCAL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Angluin (1980)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Linial (1992)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Naor and Stockmeyer (1993)</a></cite>).
This fact was established by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Loukas (2020)</a></cite> and in turn used to give upper- and lower-bounds on the size of GNNs needed to solve various graph problems (e.g., subgraph detection).
The GNN/CONGEST equivalence is almost immediate, since the communication network is fixed by the input graph in both models, and the model definitions are semantically and syntactically similar.
In contrast, while the layered representations of transformers are reminiscent of the alternation between communication and computation rounds in MPC, the “communication patterns” themselves are dynamic, and this dynamism greatly complicates the simulation of MPC algorithms by either standard transformers or ANNA-transformers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The ANNA mechanism that we study is inspired by many prior efficient attention mechanisms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kitaev et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zandieh et al., 2023</a>, e.g.,)</cite> based on locality-sensitive hashing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Indyk and Motwani (1998)</a></cite>, which is one of the key techniques for nearest neighbor search.
Some of these mechanisms have guarantees about the quality of approximation under structural assumptions on the attention matrix they are meant to approximate.
The motivation of our analysis is largely orthogonal: we instead seek to characterize the representational power of ANNA-transformers in terms of other well-understood models of parallel computation.
We further compare the capabilities of LSH-based efficient attention to other sub-quadratic alternatives, including those based on low-rank approximations of self-attention matrices <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Choromanski et al. (2021)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kacham et al. (2024)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Standard attention and transformers</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first define the (standard) attention mechanism and transformers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Attention)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A <em id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">(standard) attention head</em> <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{Attn}_{Q,K,V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>Attn</mtext><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{Attn}_{Q,K,V}</annotation></semantics></math> is specified by query, key, value embedding functions <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q,K,V:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q,K,V:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>.
On input <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, it computes</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{Attn}_{Q,K,V}(X):=\textup{softmax}(Q(X)K(X)^{{\scriptscriptstyle{\mathsf{T}}}})V(X)\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times m}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>Attn</mtext><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mtext>softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">𝖳</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{Attn}_{Q,K,V}(X):=\textup{softmax}(Q(X)K(X)^{{\scriptscriptstyle{\mathsf{T}}}})V(X)\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times m},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>, and <span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_upright">softmax</span> are applied row-wise.
We say <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is the <em id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">context length</em>, and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is the <em id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">embedding dimension</em>.
The rows of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(X)</annotation></semantics></math> (resp., <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(X)</annotation></semantics></math>) are the <em id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">queries</em> (resp., <em id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">keys</em>, <em id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">values</em>).
If <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}=Q(X)_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}=Q(X)_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{j}=K(X)_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{j}=K(X)_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{j}=V(X)_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{j}=V(X)_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th row of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{Attn}_{Q,K,V}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>Attn</mtext><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{Attn}_{Q,K,V}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{Attn}_{Q,K,V}(X)_{i}=\sum_{j=1}^{N}w_{i,j}v_{j},\quad\text{where}\quad w_{i,j}=\frac{\exp(\langle q_{i},k_{j}\rangle)}{\sum_{j^{\prime}=1}^{N}\exp(\langle q_{i},k_{j^{\prime}}\rangle)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>Attn</mtext><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">where</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{Attn}_{Q,K,V}(X)_{i}=\sum_{j=1}^{N}w_{i,j}v_{j},\quad\text{where}\quad w_{i,j}=\frac{\exp(\langle q_{i},k_{j}\rangle)}{\sum_{j^{\prime}=1}^{N}\exp(\langle q_{i},k_{j^{\prime}}\rangle)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">An <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>-headed <em id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">attention layer</em> <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon\mathbb{R}^{N\times d}\to\mathbb{R}^{N\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon\mathbb{R}^{N\times d}\to\mathbb{R}^{N\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of attention heads <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\textup{Attn}_{Q_{h},K_{h},V_{h}})_{h=1}^{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mtext>Attn</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>H</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\textup{Attn}_{Q_{h},K_{h},V_{h}})_{h=1}^{H}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\times d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\times d</annotation></semantics></math> matrices <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(W_{h})_{h=1}^{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>H</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(W_{h})_{h=1}^{H}</annotation></semantics></math>; it computes
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(X):=\sum_{h=1}^{H}\textup{Attn}_{Q_{h},K_{h},V_{h}}(X)W_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>H</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mtext>Attn</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(X):=\sum_{h=1}^{H}\textup{Attn}_{Q_{h},K_{h},V_{h}}(X)W_{h}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Transformer)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">An <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-layer <em id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">transformer</em> <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is specified by attention layers <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1},\ldots,f_{L}:\mathbb{R}^{N\times d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{N\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1},\ldots,f_{L}:\mathbb{R}^{N\times d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{N\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> and an output function <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Given input <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, define</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{(0)}:=X\quad\text{and}\quad X^{(\ell)}:=f_{\ell}(X^{(\ell-1)})\quad\text{for $\ell=1,\dotsc,L$}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>:=</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">for </mtext><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{(0)}:=X\quad\text{and}\quad X^{(\ell)}:=f_{\ell}(X^{(\ell-1)})\quad\text{for $\ell=1,\dotsc,L$}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The output of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> on input <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(X^{(L)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(X^{(L)})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> being applied row-wise.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we consider the context length <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> (the number of input tokens) as the principal scaling parameter.
This reflects the modern paradigm of long-context LLMs, where the context-length can exceed <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>6</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{6}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">GeminiTeam et al. (2023)</a></cite>, enabling book-length textual inputs.
Consequently, we typically want the size parameters <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> to be sub-linear in <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> (and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> ideally constant).
The <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(N^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(N^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> runtime of attention therefore remains the main bottleneck of the transformer architecture.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanford et al. (2024c)</a></cite>, we allow the element-wise operations (<math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{h},K_{h},V_{h},\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{h},K_{h},V_{h},\psi</annotation></semantics></math>) to be arbitrary functions <span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            Such assumptions are necessary for establishing the equivalence with the MPC model, since the MPC model allows arbitrary computation on the local memory for each machine. That said, many concrete MPC algorithms do have a simple local algorithm which can be simulated by a small MLP.</span></span></span> (limited only by bit-precision; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanford et al., 2024c</a>, Appendix A.1)</cite>).
Therefore, we can view a transformer as a computational model that alternates between arbitrary per-token computation, and communication between tokens.
This motivates a connection to massively parallel computation, defined next.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Massively Parallel Computation</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Massively Parallel Computation (MPC) framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Im et al. (2023)</a></cite> models computation on large inputs by a distributed computing system that alternates between rounds of local computation and rounds of restricted all-to-all communication.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We formally state the definition of MPC protocols (with sublinear memory) as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(MPC)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For constants <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma,\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma,\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, an <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-round <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\gamma,\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\gamma,\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>-MPC protocol specifies the following computation on inputs of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> words (where a word is <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=\Theta(\log N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=\Theta(\log N)</annotation></semantics></math> bits, represented by an element of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}_{2^{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}_{2^{p}}</annotation></semantics></math>) by <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=\Theta(N^{1+\gamma-\varepsilon})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=\Theta(N^{1+\gamma-\varepsilon})</annotation></semantics></math> machines, each with local memory <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=O(N^{\varepsilon})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>ε</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=O(N^{\varepsilon})</annotation></semantics></math> words:</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Initially, the input is arbitrarily distributed across the first </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lceil{\frac{N}{s}}\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mfrac><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lceil{\frac{N}{s}}\rceil</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> machines.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In each round, each machine prepares, as an arbitrary function of its local memory, messages to send to other machines.
The total size of messages prepared by any machine is at most </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> words.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">At the end of each round, the messages are placed in the local memory of the intended recipients.
The protocol ensures that the messages received by any machine has total size at most </span><math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> words.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">After the </span><math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-th round, the output is stored in the memory of the first </span><math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lceil{\frac{N}{s}}\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mfrac><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lceil{\frac{N}{s}}\rceil</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> machines.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We say an MPC protocol <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> computes a function <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbb{Z}_{2^{p}}^{N}\rightarrow\mathbb{Z}_{2^{p}}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi></msup><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi></msup><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbb{Z}_{2^{p}}^{N}\rightarrow\mathbb{Z}_{2^{p}}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, if for any <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathbb{Z}_{2^{p}}^{N},\pi(X)=f(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi></msup><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathbb{Z}_{2^{p}}^{N},\pi(X)=f(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is the output of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> with the input <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The primary measure of complexity considered in this paper is the number of rounds <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>.
The round complexity of numerous classical algorithmic problems is well understood.
For example, there are simple MPC protocols for
graph connectivity (<math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\log N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\log N)</annotation></semantics></math> rounds)
and sorting (<math id="S2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1)</annotation></semantics></math> rounds) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ghaffari (2019)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Approximate Nearest Neighbor Attention</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we introduce Approximate Nearest Neighbor Attention (ANNA), an attention mechanism inspired by the approximate nearest neighbor (ANN) search problem. We first outline the approximate nearest neighbor search problem and present locality-sensitive hashing (LSH), a core technique for ANN (<a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Approximate nearest neighbor and locality sensitive hashing ‣ 3 Approximate Nearest Neighbor Attention ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>). We then formally define ANNA and provide a sub-quadratic time algorithm based on LSH for computing ANNA with theoretical guarantees (<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Transformer based on Approximate Nearest Neighbor Attention ‣ 3 Approximate Nearest Neighbor Attention ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Approximate nearest neighbor and locality sensitive hashing</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first define the Approximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN) search problem.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(ANN search problem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kushilevitz et al. (1998)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Indyk and Motwani (1998)</a></cite>)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given a dataset <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> points lying in a metric space <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> and parameters <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c,r&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c,r&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, build a data structure that, given a query <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in Y</annotation></semantics></math> within distance at most <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, returns any point in <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> that is within distance <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="cr" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">cr</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the modern machine learning setting, we want to develop fast algorithms for ANN search in the high-dimensional metric space with sub-linear query time. A well-known tool that achieves this runtime and provable approximation guarantees is locality sensitive hashing (LSH).
For simplicity, we assume the metric space is <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional Euclidean space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Locality Sensitive Hashing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Indyk and Motwani (1998)</a></cite>)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix a parameter <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, an approximation factor <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> and a set <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>. Then a family <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> of hash functions <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h:\mathbb{R}^{m}\rightarrow U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h:\mathbb{R}^{m}\rightarrow U</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r,cr,p_{1},p_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r,cr,p_{1},p_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>-sensitive if the following holds for any <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y\in\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y\in\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">if </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|x-y\|\leq r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|x-y\|\leq r</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Pr_{h\in\mathcal{H}}[h(x)=h(y)]\geq p_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Pr</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr_{h\in\mathcal{H}}[h(x)=h(y)]\geq p_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">if </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|x-y\|&gt;cr" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|x-y\|&gt;cr</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Pr_{h\in\mathcal{H}}[h(x)=h(y)]\leq p_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Pr</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr_{h\in\mathcal{H}}[h(x)=h(y)]\leq p_{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The family <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> is called an LSH family with quality <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=\frac{\log(1/p_{1})}{\log(1/p_{2})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=\frac{\log(1/p_{1})}{\log(1/p_{2})}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A typical LSH-based algorithm can solve the ANN search problem with space <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(N^{1+\rho})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(N^{1+\rho})</annotation></semantics></math> and query time <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(N^{\rho})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(N^{\rho})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> can be as small as <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/c^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/c^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Andoni and Indyk (2006)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Transformer based on Approximate Nearest Neighbor Attention</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first define a family of models where only tokens with sufficiently “neighborly” queries and keys attend to one another and then provide an efficient implementation of a subset of this class.
ANNA attention units treat attention query vectors as queries to approximate nearest neighbor search and key vectors as data points.
ANNA retrieves and weights value vectors according to approximate nearest neighbor thresholds.
The following definition formalizes this family of models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(ANN Attention)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">An <em id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">Approximate Nearest Neighbor Attention (ANNA)</em> mechanism <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{ANNA}_{Q,K,V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>ANNA</mtext><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{ANNA}_{Q,K,V}</annotation></semantics></math> with query, key, and value embedding functions <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q,K,V:\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q,K,V:\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and (non-negative) parameters <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r,c,\ell,\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r,c,\ell,\eta</annotation></semantics></math>, is a (possibly randomized) mechanism that performs the following computation on an input <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{ANNA}_{Q,K,V}(X)_{i}:=\sum_{j=1}^{N}w_{i,j}v_{j}\quad\text{for all $i\in[N]$}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>ANNA</mtext><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">for all </mtext><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{ANNA}_{Q,K,V}(X)_{i}:=\sum_{j=1}^{N}w_{i,j}v_{j}\quad\text{for all $i\in[N]$},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for some non-negative weights <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i,j}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i,j}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{j}w_{i,j}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{j}w_{i,j}=1</annotation></semantics></math> that satisfy the following.
With probability at least <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\eta</annotation></semantics></math>, for all <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[N]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[N]</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<ul id="S3.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i,j}&gt;0\Rightarrow k_{j}\in\mathcal{N}(q_{i},cr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i,j}&gt;0\Rightarrow k_{j}\in\mathcal{N}(q_{i},cr)</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{j}\in\mathcal{N}(q_{i},r)\Rightarrow w_{i,j}\geq\frac{1}{(|\mathcal{N}(q_{i},cr)|-1)\ell+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{j}\in\mathcal{N}(q_{i},r)\Rightarrow w_{i,j}\geq\frac{1}{(|\mathcal{N}(q_{i},cr)|-1)\ell+1}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}:=Q(X)_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}:=Q(X)_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}:=K(X)_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}:=K(X)_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}:=V(X)_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}:=V(X)_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(q,t):=\{k\in\{k_{j}\}_{j=1}^{N}:\|q-k\|\leq t\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(q,t):=\{k\in\{k_{j}\}_{j=1}^{N}:\|q-k\|\leq t\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We define <em id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">ANNA layers</em> and <em id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p3.1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">ANNA transformers</em> in a completely analogous fashion as (standard) attention layers and transformers are defined (<a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Definition 2.1 (Attention). ‣ 2.1 Standard attention and transformers ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definitions</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> and <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Definition 2.2 (Transformer). ‣ 2.1 Standard attention and transformers ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The parameters <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r,c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r,c</annotation></semantics></math> have the same semantics as in ANN search.
The parameter <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> captures how much “attention weight” is spread over keys that are not <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-near neighbors of a query.
The failure probability <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> allows for randomization, which is typical of ANN search algorithms like LSH.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The above definition represents a set of constraints that all ANNA units must satisfy rather than a specific algorithmic implementation.
As a result, a wide variety of models satisfy the definition, including softmax attention with bounded query and key vectors, and <em id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Exact-Match Attention (EMA)</em> where <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i,j}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i,j}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}=k_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}=k_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Not all such models admit computationally efficient implementations.
To identify sub-quadratic ANNA models, we present an LSH-based implementation of ANNA that computes satisfying weight vectors <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> for specific choices of parameters <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r,c,\ell,\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r,c,\ell,\eta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We define a hash function family
<math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="G=\{g:p\in\mathbb{R}^{d}\mapsto(h_{1}(p),h_{2}(p),\ldots,h_{z}(p))\in U^{z}\ \mid\ h_{i}\in\mathcal{H},\ \forall i\in[z]\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>z</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.667em">∣</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=\{g:p\in\mathbb{R}^{d}\mapsto(h_{1}(p),h_{2}(p),\ldots,h_{z}(p))\in U^{z}\ \mid\ h_{i}\in\mathcal{H},\ \forall i\in[z]\}</annotation></semantics></math>
and sample <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> hash functions, <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{1},\ldots,g_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{1},\ldots,g_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>, from <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> independently and uniformly at random, giving <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> hash tables. Each hash code corresponds to a hash bucket in the hash table and each hash bucket maintains a sum of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>’s and count of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>’s that falls into this bucket. We preprocess all the key, value pairs by storing them in the hash tables. For each <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k_{i},v_{i}),i\in[N]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k_{i},v_{i}),i\in[N]</annotation></semantics></math>, compute the hash codes of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{1}(k_{i}),g_{2}(k_{i}),\ldots,g_{\ell}(k_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{1}(k_{i}),g_{2}(k_{i}),\ldots,g_{\ell}(k_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, and update the sum and count for the buckets corresponding to <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{1}(k_{i}),g_{2}(k_{i}),\ldots,g_{\ell}(k_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{1}(k_{i}),g_{2}(k_{i}),\ldots,g_{\ell}(k_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> respectively. For each query <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i},i\in[N]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i},i\in[N]</annotation></semantics></math>, search and retrieve all the values and counts from <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{1}(q_{i}),g_{2}(q_{i}),\ldots,g_{\ell}(q_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{1}(q_{i}),g_{2}(q_{i}),\ldots,g_{\ell}(q_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then compute the averaged value by summing up all the values in the <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> buckets, divided by the sum of counts. See Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3.2 Transformer based on Approximate Nearest Neighbor Attention ‣ 3 Approximate Nearest Neighbor Attention ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> for the details.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> ANNA implementation with LSH family <math id="alg1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> hash tables, and <math id="alg1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> hash functions/table</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
            Input <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
            ANNA output for each of the query.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
            Let <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}=Q(X)_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}=Q(X)_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}=K(X)_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}=K(X)_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="alg1.l3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}=V(X)_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}=V(X)_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="alg1.l3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[N]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[N]</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg1.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span> <span id="alg1.l4.5" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l4.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Preprocessing phase</span>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
              Sample <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> hash functions <math id="alg1.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{u,1},h_{u,2},\dots,h_{u,z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{u,1},h_{u,2},\dots,h_{u,z}</annotation></semantics></math> i.i.d. from <math id="alg1.l5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              
              Create empty hash table <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> indexed by hash codes (below).

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> each key-value pair <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k_{j},v_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k_{j},v_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
              
              
               Compute hash code <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{u}(k_{j})=(h_{u,1}(k_{j}),h_{u,2}(k_{j}),\dots,h_{u,z}(k_{j}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{u}(k_{j})=(h_{u,1}(k_{j}),h_{u,2}(k_{j}),\dots,h_{u,z}(k_{j}))</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
              
              
               <span id="alg1.l9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{u}[g_{u}(k_{j})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{u}[g_{u}(k_{j})]</annotation></semantics></math> is empty, <span id="alg1.l9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span> <math id="alg1.l9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{u}[g_{u}(k_{j})]:=(v_{j},1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{u}[g_{u}(k_{j})]:=(v_{j},1)</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l9.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">else</span> <math id="alg1.l9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{u}[g_{u}(k_{j})]\mathrel{+}=(v_{j},1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.056em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">+</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{u}[g_{u}(k_{j})]\mathrel{+}=(v_{j},1)</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
              
              
            Initialize a dictionary <math id="alg1.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textbf{attn}\leftarrow\{(q_{1},0),(q_{2},0),\ldots,(q_{N},0)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">attn</mtext><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textbf{attn}\leftarrow\{(q_{1},0),(q_{2},0),\ldots,(q_{N},0)\}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> each query <math id="alg1.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span> <span id="alg1.l11.5" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l11.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Query phase</span>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\text{sum}}\leftarrow 0;\text{count}\leftarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mtext>sum</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>;</mo><mrow><mtext>count</mtext><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\text{sum}}\leftarrow 0;\text{count}\leftarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">13:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg1.l13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l13.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l14.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">14:</span></span>
              
              
               Compute hash code <math id="alg1.l14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{u}(q_{i})=(h_{u,1}(q_{i}),\dots,h_{u,z}(q_{i}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{u}(q_{i})=(h_{u,1}(q_{i}),\dots,h_{u,z}(q_{i}))</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l15" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l15.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">15:</span></span>
              
              
               <span id="alg1.l15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="alg1.l15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{u}[g_{u}(q_{i})]=(v,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{u}[g_{u}(q_{i})]=(v,a)</annotation></semantics></math> is not empty, <span id="alg1.l15.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>
<math id="alg1.l15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\text{sum}}\mathrel{+}=v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mtext>sum</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">+</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\text{sum}}\mathrel{+}=v</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="alg1.l15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{count}\mathrel{+}=a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>count</mtext><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">+</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{count}\mathrel{+}=a</annotation></semantics></math>
  
</div>
<div id="alg1.l16" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l16.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">16:</span></span>
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textbf{attn}[q_{i}]\leftarrow v_{\text{sum}}/\text{count}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">attn</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mtext>sum</mtext></msub><mo>/</mo><mtext>count</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textbf{attn}[q_{i}]\leftarrow v_{\text{sum}}/\text{count}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l17" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l17.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">17:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l17.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <span id="alg1.l17.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">attn</span>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(LSH algorithm guarantee for ANNA)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;\sqrt{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;\sqrt{3}</annotation></semantics></math>,
LSH family <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> that is <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r,cr,p_{1},p_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r,cr,p_{1},p_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>-sensitive with quality <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho&lt;1/3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho&lt;1/3</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=\Theta(N^{3\rho}\log N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=\Theta(N^{3\rho}\log N)</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=\Theta(\log_{1/p_{2}}N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=\Theta(\log_{1/p_{2}}N)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <a href="#alg1" title="In 3.2 Transformer based on Approximate Nearest Neighbor Attention ‣ 3 Approximate Nearest Neighbor Attention ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (with <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>) implements an ANNA mechanism with parameters <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r,c,\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r,c,\ell</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=\smash{O(1/N^{1-3\rho})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=\smash{O(1/N^{1-3\rho})}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We leave the full proof of <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 3.4 (LSH algorithm guarantee for ANNA). ‣ 3.2 Transformer based on Approximate Nearest Neighbor Attention ‣ 3 Approximate Nearest Neighbor Attention ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> to <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Proof of ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.
The total runtime of <a href="#alg1" title="In 3.2 Transformer based on Approximate Nearest Neighbor Attention ‣ 3 Approximate Nearest Neighbor Attention ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(mN^{1+3\rho}\log_{1/p_{2}}N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(mN^{1+3\rho}\log_{1/p_{2}}N)</annotation></semantics></math>, assuming sampling from the LSH family and evaluating a hash function requires <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(m)</annotation></semantics></math> time and the numerical inputs to <a href="#alg1" title="In 3.2 Transformer based on Approximate Nearest Neighbor Attention ‣ 3 Approximate Nearest Neighbor Attention ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> are specified with <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=\Theta(\log N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=\Theta(\log N)</annotation></semantics></math> bits of precision.
The total space used is <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\smash{\tilde{O}(mN^{1+3\rho})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\smash{\tilde{O}(mN^{1+3\rho})}</annotation></semantics></math> bits.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The memory complexity of <a href="#alg1" title="In 3.2 Transformer based on Approximate Nearest Neighbor Attention ‣ 3 Approximate Nearest Neighbor Attention ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> can be further improved to <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\smash{\tilde{O}(mN)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\smash{\tilde{O}(mN)}</annotation></semantics></math> bits with the same time complexity by storing only one hash table with each entry keeping track of the value for each query. See <a href="#alg2" title="In Runtime and memory usage. ‣ Appendix A Proof of ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> for the detailed implementation in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Proof of ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.6</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The weight <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> depends on the number of hash collisions between <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, and is typically a function of the distance <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta:=\|q_{i}-k_{j}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta:=\|q_{i}-k_{j}\|</annotation></semantics></math>. For example, if we use the random hyperplane LSH from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Andoni et al. (2015)</a></cite>, then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="w_{i,j}\propto\exp(-\Delta^{2}\log(m)/(4-\Delta^{2}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∝</mo><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i,j}\propto\exp(-\Delta^{2}\log(m)/(4-\Delta^{2}))</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In the remainder of this paper, our ANNA-based constructions are meant to refer to their efficient implementation by <a href="#alg1" title="In 3.2 Transformer based on Approximate Nearest Neighbor Attention ‣ 3 Approximate Nearest Neighbor Attention ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> with a suitable choice of <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> and arbitrarily large <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Efficient transformers and MPC</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We prove a sharp equivalence between ANNA-transformer and MPC in the regime of sub-linear local memory and sub-quadratic number of machines (<a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 ANNA-transformer can simulate MPC ‣ 4 Efficient transformers and MPC ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sections</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> and <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 MPC can simulate ANNA-transformer ‣ 4 Efficient transformers and MPC ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>).
We also show that
ANNA subsumes alternative low-rank sub-quadratic attention mechanisms.
(<a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 ANNA-transformer can simulate low-rank transformers ‣ 4 Efficient transformers and MPC ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>ANNA-transformer can simulate MPC</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The following theorem shows that any <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-round MPC protocol with sub-linear local memory can be simulated by an ANNA-transformer with <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(R)</annotation></semantics></math> layers and sub-linear number of heads and embedding dimension.
The full proof of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1 (ANNA simulates MPC). ‣ 4.1 ANNA-transformer can simulate MPC ‣ 4 Efficient transformers and MPC ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> is in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B ANNA-transformer can simulate MPC ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(ANNA simulates MPC)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix constants <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\varepsilon&lt;\varepsilon^{\prime}&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\varepsilon&lt;\varepsilon^{\prime}&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>.
For any deterministic <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-round <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\varepsilon,\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\varepsilon,\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>-MPC protocol <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>,
there exists an ANNA-transformer <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> with
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=O(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=O(R)</annotation></semantics></math> layers,
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=O(N^{(\varepsilon^{\prime}-\varepsilon)/4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=O(N^{(\varepsilon^{\prime}-\varepsilon)/4})</annotation></semantics></math> heads per layer,
and embedding dimension <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=O(N^{\varepsilon^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=O(N^{\varepsilon^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math>,
such that <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(\mathtt{input})=\pi(\mathtt{input})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝚒𝚗𝚙𝚞𝚝</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝚒𝚗𝚙𝚞𝚝</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(\mathtt{input})=\pi(\mathtt{input})</annotation></semantics></math>
for all <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{input}\in\mathbb{Z}_{2^{p}}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚒𝚗𝚙𝚞𝚝</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi></msup><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{input}\in\mathbb{Z}_{2^{p}}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof sketch.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">In fact, we show that the special case of ANNA-transformer whose approximation factor <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0</annotation></semantics></math> is already suffice to simulate MPC. In such case, ANNA for each query is equivalent to finding only the keys that exactly match the query; we call this Exact-Match Attention (EMA), and formally define it in <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B ANNA-transformer can simulate MPC ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">In our simulation, we treat each input token as the local machine, and all the local computation is handled by the element-wise functions <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q,K,V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q,K,V</annotation></semantics></math>. The bulk of the proof is to handle the message delivery between machines using EMA.
By Proposition 24 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanford et al. (2024a)</a></cite>, we can assume that each machine only sends messages to at most <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=O(N^{\delta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>δ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=O(N^{\delta})</annotation></semantics></math> machines for some <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&lt;\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&lt;\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">We assign a unique positional encoding or identifier to each machine, and this encoding serves as a unique key to retrieve the message in each machine. The high level idea is to create a query for each machine and a key for each destination machine and the associated value is the embedding of the message sent to the destination machine in the protocol. Since each machine can send at most <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> messages to other machines, we create <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> EMA heads and each head is responsible for one outgoing message for all the <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> machines. Each machine retrieves the message sent to them by having a query in each head. Since the messages are averaged together, we use the same embedding mechanism from Lemma 3.2 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanford et al. (2024c)</a></cite> to allow error correction in the element-wise operations.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">This gives us a sub-quadratic time reduction from MPC to ANNA-transformer: i.e., the communication process can be implemented in near linear time, whereas it is quadratic for standard attention. In addition, this ties ANNA-transformer in the existing MPC hierarchy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanford et al. (2024a)</a></cite>: any problem solvable by an <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-round, <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(N^{\varepsilon})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>ε</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(N^{\varepsilon})</annotation></semantics></math>-memory MPC protocol can be solved by <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(R)</annotation></semantics></math>-layer ANNA-transformer with <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="mH=O(N^{\varepsilon+\delta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">mH=O(N^{\varepsilon+\delta})</annotation></semantics></math>, for some <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. For example, following Theorem 3.1 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hajiaghayi et al. (2019)</a></cite>, <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1)</annotation></semantics></math>-layer ANNA-transformer can solve 3-SUM with <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="mH=O(N^{{1/2+\delta}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">mH=O(N^{{1/2+\delta}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>MPC can simulate ANNA-transformer</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The following theorem (proved in <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C MPC can Simulate ANNA-transformer ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>) shows that any <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-layer ANNA-transformer (as implemented by <a href="#alg1" title="In 3.2 Transformer based on Approximate Nearest Neighbor Attention ‣ 3 Approximate Nearest Neighbor Attention ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) can be simulated by a <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(L)</annotation></semantics></math>-round MPC protocol.
Since <a href="#alg1" title="In 3.2 Transformer based on Approximate Nearest Neighbor Attention ‣ 3 Approximate Nearest Neighbor Attention ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is randomized, it uses a random seed to sample the hash functions from the LSH family.
The simulation assumes access to the random seeds needed for all layers in the ANNA-transformer.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(MPC simulates ANNA)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix constants <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\varepsilon&lt;\varepsilon^{\prime}&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\varepsilon&lt;\varepsilon^{\prime}&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>.
For any <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-layer ANNA-transformer <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> (as implemented by <a href="#alg1" title="In 3.2 Transformer based on Approximate Nearest Neighbor Attention ‣ 3 Approximate Nearest Neighbor Attention ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) with <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="mH=O(N^{\varepsilon})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>ε</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">mH=O(N^{\varepsilon})</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(L/(\varepsilon^{\prime}-\varepsilon))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(L/(\varepsilon^{\prime}-\varepsilon))</annotation></semantics></math>-round MPC protocol <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> with local memory <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=O(N^{\varepsilon^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=O(N^{\varepsilon^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=O(N^{1+\varepsilon-\varepsilon^{\prime}+\nicefrac{{3}}{{c^{2}}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mpadded voffset="0.3em"><mn mathsize="0.710em">3</mn></mpadded><mpadded style="width:-0.15em;" lspace="-0.1em" width="-0.15em"><mo stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo></mpadded><msup><mi mathsize="0.710em">c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=O(N^{1+\varepsilon-\varepsilon^{\prime}+\nicefrac{{3}}{{c^{2}}}})</annotation></semantics></math> machines such that
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(\mathtt{input})=T(\mathtt{input})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝚒𝚗𝚙𝚞𝚝</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝚒𝚗𝚙𝚞𝚝</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(\mathtt{input})=T(\mathtt{input})</annotation></semantics></math> for all
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{input}\in\mathbb{Z}_{2^{p}}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚒𝚗𝚙𝚞𝚝</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi></msup><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{input}\in\mathbb{Z}_{2^{p}}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Observe that the number of machines used in the simulation of the ANNA-transformer can be strongly sub-quadratic (and in fact, close to linear when <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> is large).
In contrast, the simulation of a standard transformer from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanford et al. (2024c)</a></cite> requires <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> machines.
As previously discussed (<a href="#S1.SS2" title="1.2 Our contributions ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>), this shows that ANNA-transformer more sharply characterizes efficient MPC protocols than standard transformers do.
On the other hand, by <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 4.2 (MPC simulates ANNA). ‣ 4.2 MPC can simulate ANNA-transformer ‣ 4 Efficient transformers and MPC ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, round-complexity lower bounds for MPC directly imply depth lower bounds for ANNA-transformers.
This argument was used in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanford et al. (2024c)</a></cite> to establish (conditional) depth lower bounds for standard transformers on problems such as graph connectivity and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-hop induction heads; these lower bounds also hold for ANNA-transformers.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>ANNA-transformer can simulate low-rank transformers</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As mentioned in <a href="#S1" title="1 Introduction ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, there are many proposals for efficient attention alternatives.
In this section, we focus on the sub-quadratic alternatives based on low-rank approximations of the attention matrix.
Specifically, we ask the following: <em id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">what problems are intrinsically easy for ANNA but hard for low-rank approximation attention, and vice versa?</em></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Low-rank attention)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A </span>low-rank attention<span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is specified by two feature maps <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\prime},K^{\prime}:\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\prime},K^{\prime}:\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\ll N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≪</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\ll N</annotation></semantics></math> (with the intention of approximating <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{softmax}(Q(X)K(X)^{{\scriptscriptstyle{\mathsf{T}}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">𝖳</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{softmax}(Q(X)K(X)^{{\scriptscriptstyle{\mathsf{T}}}})</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\prime}(X)K^{\prime}(X)^{{\scriptscriptstyle{\mathsf{T}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">𝖳</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\prime}(X)K^{\prime}(X)^{{\scriptscriptstyle{\mathsf{T}}}}</annotation></semantics></math>).
On input <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, it computes <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\prime}(X)K^{\prime}(X)^{\scriptscriptstyle{\mathsf{T}}}V(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">𝖳</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\prime}(X)K^{\prime}(X)^{\scriptscriptstyle{\mathsf{T}}}V(X)</annotation></semantics></math> by first computing <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{\prime}(X)^{{\scriptscriptstyle{\mathsf{T}}}}V(X)\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">𝖳</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{\prime}(X)^{{\scriptscriptstyle{\mathsf{T}}}}V(X)\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times m}</annotation></semantics></math>, and then left-multiplying by <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\prime}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\prime}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Note that <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanford et al. (2024c)</a></cite> gives a lower bound of any low-rank attention for the <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-hop problem.
Later in <a href="#S5" title="5 ANNA-transformer for reasoning tasks ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we give a construction of <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\log k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\log k)</annotation></semantics></math>-depth ANNA-transformer solving <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-hop, and this directly gives a type of problem that is easy for ANNA but hard for low-rank attention.
However, is there any problem that is easy for low-rank attention but hard for ANNA?</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The following theorem answers the question by showing any <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-layer low-rank attention-transformer can be simulated by a <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(L)</annotation></semantics></math>-layer ANNA-transformer.
So, under the time and parameter-efficient regime (sub-linear rank and embedding dimension), low-rank attention-transformer is no stronger than ANNA-transformer.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.4</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(ANNA simulates low-rank attention)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For constants <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\varepsilon&lt;\varepsilon^{\prime}&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\varepsilon&lt;\varepsilon^{\prime}&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, any </span>low-rank attention<span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> based transformer with depth <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, rank <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, embedding dimension <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="rm=O(N^{\varepsilon})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>ε</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">rm=O(N^{\varepsilon})</annotation></semantics></math> can be simulated by an ANNA-transformer with depth <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(L)</annotation></semantics></math>, number of heads <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=O(N^{(\varepsilon^{\prime}-\varepsilon)/4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=O(N^{(\varepsilon^{\prime}-\varepsilon)/4})</annotation></semantics></math> and embedding dimension <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=O(N^{\varepsilon^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=O(N^{\varepsilon^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We prove <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4.4 (ANNA simulates low-rank attention). ‣ 4.3 ANNA-transformer can simulate low-rank transformers ‣ 4 Efficient transformers and MPC ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a> by first using <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(L/(\varepsilon^{\prime}-\varepsilon))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(L/(\varepsilon^{\prime}-\varepsilon))</annotation></semantics></math>-round MPC to simulate <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-layer low-rank transformer, and then <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1 (ANNA simulates MPC). ‣ 4.1 ANNA-transformer can simulate MPC ‣ 4 Efficient transformers and MPC ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> give us the simulation of <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-layer low-rank transformer with ANNA-transformer through MPC.
The full proof is given in <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D ANN/EM Attention can simulate low-rank Attention via MPC ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Other efficient attention mechanisms based on nearest neighbor search.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Reformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kitaev et al. (2020)</a></cite> is another efficient attention based on LSH.
In Reformer, the input tokens are sorted by their (scalar) hash values.
Then, this sorted list is split into equal-sized chunks, each containing only <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1)</annotation></semantics></math>-many tokens.
Standard attention is applied within each chunk.
We show that the expressive power of Reformer must come from the sorting operation: without sorting, the restriction of attention within each constant-size chunk prevents Reformer from even computing basic functions like “average” with <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1)</annotation></semantics></math> layers (regardless of the embedding dimension); details are given in <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Discussion on Reformer ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">KDEformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zandieh et al. (2023)</a></cite> and HyperAttention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han et al. (2024)</a></cite> approximate softmax attention matrices as a sum of a sparse matrix and a low-rank matrix.
They use
LSH techniques to find sparse elements (i.e., heavy elements in the attention matrix) and low-rank attention for the remaining components.
<a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4.4 (ANNA simulates low-rank attention). ‣ 4.3 ANNA-transformer can simulate low-rank transformers ‣ 4 Efficient transformers and MPC ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a> indicates this low-rank part does not substantially increase the representational power.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>ANNA-transformer for reasoning tasks</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we study ANNA-transformer on two concrete reasoning tasks: Match2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanford et al. (2023)</a></cite> and <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-hop <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanford et al. (2024c)</a></cite>.
These tasks are benchmarks for evaluating the reasoning capabilities of transformers, and they separate different neural architectures in terms of their representational strengths.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>ANNA-transformer solves <span id="S5.SS1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">Match2</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">Match2</span> task <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanford et al. (2023)</a></cite> measures the ability of a model to associate paired elements with one another. We show that a single ANNA mechanism can solve Match2.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">Match2</span>)<span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given an input sequence <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{N})\in[M]^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{N})\in[M]^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\leq\poly(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>poly</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\leq\poly(N)</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th output of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{Match2}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Match2</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{Match2}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbbm{1}\{\exists j\centerdot x_{i}+x_{j}=0\bmod M\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∙</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>mod</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbbm{1}\{\exists j\centerdot x_{i}+x_{j}=0\bmod M\}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[N]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[N]</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.2</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N,M=N^{O(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N,M=N^{O(1)}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists an ANNA-transformer <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> with one layer, one attention head, and embedding dimension <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(X)=\textup{Match2}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Match2</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(X)=\textup{Match2}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in[M]^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in[M]^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>ANNA-transformer solves <math id="S5.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-hop</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The induction heads (a.k.a. associative recall) task <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Elhage et al., 2021</a>)</cite> is a reasoning task that predicts the next token by completing the most recent bigram.
It has been identified as an important mechanism for the emergent “in-context learning” ability of LLMs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 5.3</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Induction heads)<span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite alphabet and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in\Sigma^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in\Sigma^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>. For each <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[N]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[N]</annotation></semantics></math>, define</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sigma(w,i)=\max\{\{0\}\cup\{j\in\mathbb{N}:j\leq i,w_{j-1}=w_{i}\}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>max</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(w,i)=\max\{\{0\}\cup\{j\in\mathbb{N}:j\leq i,w_{j-1}=w_{i}\}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The <em id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">induction head</em> task is to compute, for each <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq N</annotation></semantics></math>, the value of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{\sigma(w,i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{\sigma(w,i)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For example, let <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma=\{\tt{a},\tt{b},\tt{c}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>𝚊</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝚋</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝚌</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma=\{\tt{a},\tt{b},\tt{c}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=\tt{aabcbabca}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝚊𝚊𝚋𝚌𝚋𝚊𝚋𝚌𝚊</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=\tt{aabcbabca}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{\sigma(w,9)}=\tt{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝚋</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{\sigma(w,9)}=\tt{b}</annotation></semantics></math> because the 9th token is <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tt{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚊</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tt{a}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the last occurrence of <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tt{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚊</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tt{a}</annotation></semantics></math> before position 9 (which is in position 6) is followed by <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tt{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚋</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tt{b}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The following theorem shows that our ANNA-transformer can solve induction heads problem using constant number of layers and sub-linear embedding dimension and number of heads.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.4</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix constants <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\varepsilon&lt;\varepsilon^{\prime}&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\varepsilon&lt;\varepsilon^{\prime}&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>.
There exists an ANNA-transformer <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=O(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=O(1)</annotation></semantics></math> layers, <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=O(N^{(\varepsilon^{\prime}-\varepsilon)/4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=O(N^{(\varepsilon^{\prime}-\varepsilon)/4})</annotation></semantics></math> heads per layer, and embedding dimension <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=O(N^{\varepsilon^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=O(N^{\varepsilon^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(w)_{i}=w_{\sigma(w,i)},\forall i\in[N]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(w)_{i}=w_{\sigma(w,i)},\forall i\in[N]</annotation></semantics></math>, for all <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in\Sigma^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in\Sigma^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We prove <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 5.4. ‣ 5.2 ANNA-transformer solves 𝑘-hop ‣ 5 ANNA-transformer for reasoning tasks ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a> (in <a href="#A6.SS2" title="F.2 ANN transformer Solves 𝑘-hop ‣ Appendix F ANNA-transformer Solves 𝑘-hop and Match2 ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.2</span></a>) by constructing a constant-round MPC algorithm for induction heads, and then applying <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1 (ANNA simulates MPC). ‣ 4.1 ANNA-transformer can simulate MPC ‣ 4 Efficient transformers and MPC ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> to convert it into an ANNA-transformer.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The induction heads task was generalized by
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanford et al. (2024c)</a></cite> to a <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-step variant called “<math id="S5.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-hop”.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 5.5</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-hop induction heads)<span id="S5.Thmtheorem5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite alphabet and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in\Sigma^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in\Sigma^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{k}(w,i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{k}(w,i)</annotation></semantics></math> denote a <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-fold composition of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(w,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(w,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> from the previous definition.
The <em id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright"><math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-hop induction head</em> task is to compute <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{\sigma^{k}(w,i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{\sigma^{k}(w,i)}</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq N</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Using the same example where <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma=\{\tt{a},\tt{b},\tt{c}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>𝚊</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝚋</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝚌</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma=\{\tt{a},\tt{b},\tt{c}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=\tt{aabcbabca}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝚊𝚊𝚋𝚌𝚋𝚊𝚋𝚌𝚊</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=\tt{aabcbabca}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=2</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{\sigma(\sigma(w,9))}=\tt{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝚊</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{\sigma(\sigma(w,9))}=\tt{a}</annotation></semantics></math>
because the last occurrence of <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tt{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚋</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tt{b}</annotation></semantics></math> before position 7 is followed by <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tt{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚊</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tt{a}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">As was done in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanford et al. (2024c)</a></cite>, we construct a <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\log k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\log k)</annotation></semantics></math>-round MPC algorithm for <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-hop using function composition, thus yielding a logarithmic depth scaling for ANNA-transformers on this task.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.6</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix constants <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\varepsilon&lt;\varepsilon^{\prime}&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\varepsilon&lt;\varepsilon^{\prime}&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, any <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and alphabet <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\Sigma|=O(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\Sigma|=O(N)</annotation></semantics></math>.
There exists an ANNA-transformer <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=O(\log k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=O(\log k)</annotation></semantics></math> layers, <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=O(N^{(\varepsilon^{\prime}-\varepsilon)/4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=O(N^{(\varepsilon^{\prime}-\varepsilon)/4})</annotation></semantics></math> heads per layer, and embedding dimension <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=O(N^{\varepsilon^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=O(N^{\varepsilon^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(w)_{i}=w_{\sigma^{k}(w,i)},\forall i\in[N]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(w)_{i}=w_{\sigma^{k}(w,i)},\forall i\in[N]</annotation></semantics></math>, for all <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in\Sigma^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in\Sigma^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The full construction is given in <a href="#A6.SS2" title="F.2 ANN transformer Solves 𝑘-hop ‣ Appendix F ANNA-transformer Solves 𝑘-hop and Match2 ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.2</span></a>.
We note that while prior works have given transformer constructions for <math id="S5.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-hop <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bietti et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanford et al. (2024c)</a></cite> (and Match2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanford et al. (2023)</a></cite>), these results do not directly imply ANNA-transformers constructions, given the differences in the architectures.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanford et al. (2024c)</a></cite> showed that multi-layer
recurrent nets and low-rank sub-quadratic attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Choromanski et al. (2021)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kacham et al. (2024)</a></cite> are unable to solve <math id="S5.SS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-hop unless the depth is <math id="S5.SS2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(k)</annotation></semantics></math> or their memory size/embedding dimension is <math id="S5.SS2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega(N/k^{6})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(N/k^{6})</annotation></semantics></math>.
On contrast, ANNA-transformer achieves both <math id="S5.SS2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\log k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\log k)</annotation></semantics></math>-depth and sublinear (in <math id="S5.SS2.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>) width.
In this sense, the <math id="S5.SS2.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-hop task separates ANNA-transformer from these other efficient neural architectures.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Experiments on Match2 and induction heads</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We empirically test the performance of ANNA-transformer on the Match2 and induction heads tasks.
Experimental details are given in <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Experimental details ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a>.
Since <a href="#alg1" title="In 3.2 Transformer based on Approximate Nearest Neighbor Attention ‣ 3 Approximate Nearest Neighbor Attention ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is not differentiable, we train a softmax version of attention as a surrogate, and then distill from the trained model to an ANNA-transformer (based on <a href="#alg1" title="In 3.2 Transformer based on Approximate Nearest Neighbor Attention ‣ 3 Approximate Nearest Neighbor Attention ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> with angular LSH <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Andoni et al. (2015)</a></cite>).
Our softmax attention normalizes all the queries and keys in <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(X)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(X)</annotation></semantics></math> to have unit norm, and computes <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{softmax}(\beta\cdot Q(X)K(X)^{{\scriptscriptstyle{\mathsf{T}}}})V(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">𝖳</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{softmax}(\beta\cdot Q(X)K(X)^{{\scriptscriptstyle{\mathsf{T}}}})V(X)</annotation></semantics></math> with a tunable temperature parameter <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The Match2 dataset is generated the same way as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kozachinskiy et al. (2025)</a></cite> with context length <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=32</annotation></semantics></math> and upper bound <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=37" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>37</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=37</annotation></semantics></math>. One-layer ANNA-transformers are able to achieve zero error with <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=8</annotation></semantics></math> hash tables and <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=1</annotation></semantics></math> hash function per table. See <a href="#S5.F1.sf1" title="In Figure 1 ‣ 5.3 Experiments on Match2 and induction heads ‣ 5 ANNA-transformer for reasoning tasks ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(a)</span></a> for the detailed performance.
For induction heads, we use the dataset from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanford et al. (2024c)</a></cite> with number of hops <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math>, context length <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=100</annotation></semantics></math> and alphabet size <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\Sigma|=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\Sigma|=4</annotation></semantics></math>.
A two-layer ANNA-transformer achieved highly nontrivial error with <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=32</annotation></semantics></math> hash tables and <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=2</annotation></semantics></math> hash functions per table; with more hash tables, the error rate was as low as <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.1</annotation></semantics></math>. See <a href="#S5.F1.sf2" title="In Figure 1 ‣ 5.3 Experiments on Match2 and induction heads ‣ 5 ANNA-transformer for reasoning tasks ‣ Fast attention mechanisms: a tale of parallelism" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(b)</span></a> for the detailed results.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F1.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.09001v1/match2.svg" id="S5.F1.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/378;" width="476" height="378"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F1.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S5.F1.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Match2 </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F1.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.09001v1/induction-head.png" id="S5.F1.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/388;" width="476" height="388" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F1.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S5.F1.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Induction heads</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S5.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">All errors are averaged over 10 runs.
(a) Error rate on Match2: x-axis denotes the number of hash tables <math id="S5.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, and different colors correspond to different numbers <math id="S5.F1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> of hash functions per hash table.
(b) Error rate on induction heads: Rows correspond to the number of hash tables in the first layer, columns correspond to the number of hash tables in the second layer. The reported error rate is the best achieved over the choice of <math id="S5.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in\{1,2,3,4\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in\{1,2,3,4\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion and future work</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we propose a more efficient class of neural architecture, the ANNA-transformers, which not only preserve the representational power of standard transformer characterized by the MPC framework but also yield a tighter equivalence with the MPC model. Furthermore, we show that constant layers of ANNA-transformers can simulate constant layers of low-rank transformer, and can solve reasoning tasks such as Match2 and <math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-hop tasks in near-optimal depth.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">There are some interesting directions we leave for future work. While our ad hoc training method was effective as a proof-of-concept, it is desirable to develop a principled training method that directly optimizes the performance of an ANNA-transformer (or a differentiable variant thereof), rather than that of a surrogate model. Also, our empirical validation was limited to small synthetic datasets; extending these experiments to large-scale, real-world benchmarks is an important next step.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Acknowledgements</h4>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Part of this work was done at the “Modern Paradigms in Generalization” and “Special Year on Large Language Models and Transformers, Part 1” programs at the Simons Institute for the Theory of Computing, Berkeley in 2024. We acknowledge support from the ONR under grants N00014-24-1-2700, N00014-22-1-2713,
from the NSF under grant CCF2008733,
and an award from the Columbia Center of AI Technology in collaboration with Amazon.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
